This is something I created to eliminate the need of using run_init when
doing daemon management, and has been used in Gentoo for a while.=20
Gentoo has a dependency based init script system, and the scripts have a
binary (/sbin/runscript) for the script interpreter.  Its there that
runscript_selinux.so is dlopened and ran, leaving us with an integrated
run_init; role transitions and typing 'run_init' isn't needed.  I added
another config file run_init_type (containing "run_init_t").  When the
current domain matches this, runscript_selinux does the pam
authentication.  Otherwise, the authentication and setexec are skipped,
since either it is already initrc_t, or labeling is wrong, and things
will break anyway (such as if current context is kernel_t or init_t).  I
also have it assume defaults if the run_init_type and initrc_context
files were missing or empty, since runscript_selinux runs even during
boot, and we don't want to fail at that point.

Runscript_selinux is executed in our /sbin/runscript thusly:

	lib_handle =3D dlopen("/lib/rcscripts/runscript_selinux.so", RTLD_NOW | RT=
LD_GLOBAL);
	if( lib_handle !=3D NULL ) {
		selinux_run_init =3D dlsym(lib_handle, "selinux_runscript");
		selinux_run_init();
	}

	[execv-related code here]

and it is compiled:
gcc -Wall -fPIC -DUSE_PAM -shared -o runscript_selinux.so
runscript_selinux.c -Wl,-soname,runscript_selinux.so  -lpam -lpam_misc
-lselinux -ldl

The only policy needed is:
domain_auto_trans(sysadm_t,initrc_exec_t,run_init_t)

